 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T21:28+08:00",
 "answer": "Most likely, they’re showing **different measures or time windows**, so the labels don’t line up exactly.\n\nIf you’re in Singapore, NEA’s **PSI reflects the past 24 hours**, while an app may show a more current PM2.5 reading or calculate its own AQI using different data and category cutoffs. A short-lived pollution spike can therefore make the app say **“unhealthy”** while the 24-hour PSI still says **“moderate.”** ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/media/readers-letters/index/pm-2-5-for-immediate-activities-psi-forecast-for-next-day))\n\nApps can also use different monitoring stations, models, or pollutants; some report just PM2.5, while others base the headline index on whichever pollutant is worst. ([aqmd.gov](https://www.aqmd.gov/home/air-quality/current-air-quality-data/aqi-information-and-faqs))\n\nCheck each reading’s **timestamp, pollutant, averaging period, and data source**. If you share the app name and your location, I can help compare the specific readings. If you’re sensitive to air pollution or you’re having symptoms, take the more cautious advice in the meantime.",
